comments.body:
@reporter While what you're saying is true, it's well beyond the scope of anything I'm willing to try as a volunteer, as it's a pretty significant redesign of some of fzf's core logic, and introduces a whole *new* set of problems, besides.  (e.g. "lots of child processes hanging around" can easily equal "you crash your computer by holding down an arrow key".)

Meanwhile, for your specific example, there's nothing stopping you from implementing that by introducing a control script that checks for an output file and pipes it to stdout, or if the file doesn't exist or is too stale, runs a daemonized background process to generate its output into that file, before copying the file contents to stdout.

If you did that, the result would be exactly what you're asking for, and it would work with the patch as it sits.  (And of course it'll be on your head if you end up running hundreds of these unkillable background processes. :<nick>:)
